.TE
.PP
Units are seconds unless named otherwise.
.I sample_every
is poll ticks between M2 samples;
.I proc_min_bytes
is the rss+swap floor a process must clear to earn an index row;
.I recent_ring
/
.I hourly_ring
are sample counts kept in each ring;
.I advise_growth_mib_per_hour
is rule 2's leak-ish growth-rate threshold;
.I swap_storm_eta_minutes
/
.I swap_storm_hysteresis_polls
govern V2.M1's early warning (see
.B WARNING
below);
.I zombie_advise_min
is how many unreaped children one parent needs before
.I advise
names it;
the
.I auto_calm_*
keys are V2.M2's policy \(em see
.B AUTO\-CALM
below.
.I auto_calm_enabled
is the master switch; even armed (see
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I autocalm
verb) and even with
.B ramstein\-autocalm.timer
enabled, a cycle does nothing while this is false.
.I notify_kill_enabled
governs V3's post-kill desktop notification (see
.B SPEAKING, NOT ACTING (V3)
below); true by default, unlike every other opt-in feature above, since a
kernel OOM-kill is a fact about the user's own machine with no
false-positive surface, not a forecast that can be wrong.
The
.I standing_*
keys govern
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I standing
verb's "stock" tier \(em how large
.RI ( standing_min_bytes ")"
and how flat
.RI ( standing_flat_pct " over " standing_window_hours ")"
a process must be to count as dead weight rather than an ordinary
resident process.
The
.I incident_*
keys govern
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I incidents
verb: swap crossing
.I incident_swap_pct
(a plateau/stock trigger, independent of
.BR WARNING 's
own rate+ETA gate below \(em a slow sustained climb can cross this
without ever tripping a swap\-storm warning) snapshots the top
.I incident_max_residents
processes by rss+swap, retaining the most recent
.I incident_max_records
crossings.
The
.I stance_*
keys govern V4's memory stance (see
.B MEMORY STANCE (V4)
below):
.I stance_protect_floor_ceiling_pct
bounds the
.B protect
tier's floor at this percent of MemTotal regardless of how much protected
usage grows, and
.I stance_expendable_squeeze_pct
sizes the
.B expendable
tier's
.I memory.high
(while hot) as this percent of a leaf's own current usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ramstein/config.json
Seeded once at install (owner_uid from the installing sudo call), never
overwritten on reinstall.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/index.db
The per-process sample ring (sqlite, WAL mode): two rings sharing one
promoted flag \(em
.I recent_ring
keeps every sample (~1h at defaults),
.I hourly_ring
keeps one promoted sample per hour (~7d). Identity key is
.RI ( pid ", " starttime )
so a reused pid can never be mistaken for the process that held it before.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/ledger.jsonl
One JSON line per
.I calm
or
.I kill
action: timestamp, verb, pid, comm, argument, result.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/kill_notify.json
V3's post-kill notification high-water mark (last-reported kill
timestamp) \(em on disk, not memory-only, so a daemon restart doesn't
re-report a kill from hours ago.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/swap_watermark.json
.I standing 's
swap tier: since-timestamp and peak bytes used, updated once per poll
tick (an in-memory compare; a disk write only when the peak actually
rises \(em on a machine that never touches swap, exactly one write,
ever).
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/incidents.jsonl
.IR incidents "'s own record: one JSON line per threshold crossing"
(swap%, PSI full, or a swap-storm warning becoming active), each
carrying the triggering value and a snapshot of the top residents by
rss+swap at that instant. Append-only; trimmed to
.I incident_max_records
on write, oldest dropped first.
.TP
.I /etc/ramstein/stance.json
The memory stance (V4): operator-authored, never written by the daemon.
Missing entirely, or present with an empty rule list, is the shipped
default \(em protects nothing, caps nothing. See
.B MEMORY STANCE (V4)
below.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ramstein/stance_touched.json
Every cgroup property the daemon has ever written under the stance
(unit, property, which road it was written with) \(em the durable list
.I stance rollback
walks to reset everything back to its off value. Survives a restart;
entries are removed as they're successfully reset.
.TP
.I /run/ramstein/status.json
0640, owned by
.IR owner_uid .
Written atomically (tmp file + rename). Carries the M2/M3 pill digest
(top process, zombie count, one advise headline) for the GNOME extension,
recomputed on the sampler's own cadence; V2.M1's
.I warning
field (swap-storm, when active) and V2.M2's
.I autocalm
field (armed state, last cycle) ride along on every poll.
.TP
.I /run/ramstein/control.sock
0660, owned by
.IR owner_uid .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Clean shutdown: the current poll loop iteration finishes, the socket is
closed.
.SH WARNING (V2.M1 \(em swap-storm early warning)
A second EWMA tracks swap consumption specifically (independent of the
main burn EWMA). When it's actively growing
.I and
the combined ETA-to-OOM crosses
.IR swap_storm_eta_minutes ,
.I status.json
gains a
.I warning
object
.RI ( kind ": " swap_storm ", " eta_oom_seconds ", " swap_burn_bps ", "
.IR top_growers ,
the top-3 processes by swap delta over a recent window). It's sticky:
.I swap_storm_hysteresis_polls
consecutive clear polls are required before it drops, so a value hovering
right at the horizon doesn't flap the pill on and off. This catches a gap
the general avail%/PSI/eta classifier can miss \(em
.I MemAvailable
is a reclaimable-cache-aware kernel heuristic that can look fine for a
while even as swap visibly drains.
.SH AUTO-CALM (V2.M2)
Arms
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I calm
machinery to act on its own, on a timer. Three independent gates, all
required before anything real happens \(em see
.B Auto\-calm
under
.B SECURITY
below for the full model. Trigger: PSI some/full avg10 crossing
.IR auto_calm_psi_some / auto_calm_psi_full ,
or (when
.I auto_calm_use_swap_storm
is true) an active swap-storm warning. Graduated response against the
current top RSS grower, each step independently toggleable:
.IR auto_calm_step_renice " (to " auto_calm_nice ")"
then
.IR auto_calm_step_squeeze " (cgroup " memory.high " to "
.I auto_calm_squeeze_pct
percent of the target's current RSS \(em always at least 110%, since
.I calm \-\-high's
own floor never allows less and a lower request would silently be a
no-op; the default headroom lets the target keep running while capping
how much further it can grow).
.I auto_calm_cooldown_seconds
rate-limits how often a cycle can act for real. There is no step past
squeeze \(em the daemon never kills anything on its own, at any setting;
it only ever surfaces a suggested
.I calm \-\-kill
command for a human to run.
.SH SPEAKING, NOT ACTING (V3)
Every finding above is otherwise available only to someone who runs
.BR ramstein (1)
by hand. When
.I notify_kill_enabled
is true (the default), a new kernel OOM-kill sends a real desktop
notification to
.I owner_uid's
own session \(em straight over D\-Bus
.RI ( "sudo \-u '#<owner_uid>' env DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/<uid>/bus notify\-send" ),
going around the GNOME pill entirely, so it reaches the desktop even on
a shell that has not reloaded in days. Best\-effort: a failure is logged
to stderr (journald, under the real unit) and never blocks the poll
loop. The high\-water mark (last\-reported kill) persists to
.IR /var/lib/ramstein/kill_notify.json ,
not memory only, so a daemon restart does not re\-report a kill from
hours ago; a fresh install bootstraps the mark to the daemon's own start
time rather than scanning the machine's entire kill history on first
boot. A burst of kills collapses to one notification naming the most
recent, never one popup per kill. This reads
.I kills
and writes nothing any other verb reads back \(em it touches neither the
kill gate nor auto\-calm's three gates above.
.SH MEMORY STANCE (V4)
Operator ruling, 2026\-09\-05: the daemon stops being a pure gauge and starts keeping a short,
operator\-authored policy applied as real cgroup memory controls, read from
.IR /etc/ramstein/stance.json .
Ships with zero rules \(em the default protects nothing and caps nothing until the operator names a
rule; this file is never written by the daemon, only read. A file that fails to load or validate
applies nothing at all: every previously\-touched cgroup is rolled back and the error is reported,
the same as a clean daemon stop.
.PP
Each rule matches a
.B leaf
cgroup scope only, by
.IR unit_glob ", " comm ", " exe_glob
(needed for anything whose
.I comm
is not a stable name), or
.IR container_glob .
First match wins; anything matching nothing is
.BR unclassified " \(em reported by " ramstein " (1)'s "
.I ledger
verb, never capped. Three tiers:
.TP
.B protect
.I memory.low
on the target leaf
.B and
every shared ancestor up through
.I user@<uid>.service
and
.IR user\-<uid>.slice ,
sized to the sum of every protect\-tier leaf's own usage and capped at
.I stance_protect_floor_ceiling_pct
of MemTotal (a leak inside a protected scope must not become an unbounded reservation) \(em a
leaf's own
.I memory.low
alone protects nothing against reclaim pressure from an ancestor with none, measured directly.
.I ManagedOOMPreference=avoid
rides along on the leaf. Never touches the top\-level
.I user.slice
or
.IR system.slice ;
a leaf outside any session (e.g. a docker scope under
.IR system.slice )
gets leaf\-level protection only.
.TP
.B expendable
No standing squeeze.
.I memory.high
rides
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I autocalm
verb's own three consent gates and squeeze mechanism as one more step, sized to
.I stance_expendable_squeeze_pct
of the leaf's own current usage, applied only while autocalm's trigger is hot and released back to
.I max
the instant it calms \(em unlike the pre\-existing top\-RSS squeeze step, this one self\-releases.
No protection; ManagedOOMPreference stays at its default.
.TP
.B cap
A standing
.I memory.high ,
fixed
.RI ( memory_high_bytes )
or relative
.RI ( memory_high_pct_of_total ),
applied every poll tick regardless of system state \(em containers are capped by design intent, not
system state.
.PP
Two write roads: a cgroup living inside a
.I user@<uid>.service's
own delegated subtree is written via
.I "sudo \-u '#<uid>' env DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=... systemctl \-\-user set\-property"
\(em the same bridge V3's desktop notification already uses, measured to be the road that sticks
without racing the owning user manager. Everything else \(em
.IR system.slice ,
docker scopes, or the two session\-level units
.I above
the delegation boundary \(em is root's own cgroup outright: a plain
.IR "systemctl set\-property" ,
no bridge.
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I ledger
verb shows which road each managed scope took.
.PP
.B ramstein stance rollback
walks every cgroup property ever written (persisted to
.IR /var/lib/ramstein/stance_touched.json ),
resets each to its off value via the same road it was written with, and reads it back to confirm; a
vanished unit counts as a pass. Runs automatically before the stance (re\-)applies on every daemon
start. A ceiling here throttles and never kills \(em kill remains exclusively a human TTY verb via
.I calm \-\-kill ,
untouched by any of this.
.SH SECURITY
The relevant attacker is an unprivileged local process abusing the root
daemon; ramsteind has no network attack surface at all \(em the only
component that ever reaches the internet is the separate, unprivileged
.BR ramstein\-update (1)
checker.
.TP
.B Socket
AF_UNIX, newline-delimited JSON, bounded reads (4 KiB line cap, 5 s
timeout). Every connection is checked against
.B SO_PEERCRED
\(em only root or the configured
.I owner_uid
may issue commands \(em on top of the socket's own 0660 file mode. Two
independent gates.
.TP
.B Hostile input
Malformed JSON, non-objects, and unknown commands are answered with
.RI { \(dqerror\(dq : ...} and the connection ends; they never crash the
daemon.
.TP
.B The kill gate
Nothing is killed without a fresh, per-invocation confirmation typed at a
real TTY \(em no config flag, no persisting
.BR \-\-yes ,
no non-interactive kill path; a non-TTY stdin is refused outright by the
CLI before it ever reaches the socket. The daemon independently
re-validates
.RI ( pid ", " starttime )
identity against live
.I /proc
before signaling \(em a stale or reused pid is refused even if the CLI
layer were somehow bypassed. PID 1, kernel threads, and the daemon's own
pid are never valid targets, hardcoded, not configurable.
.TP
.B Auto\-calm
Three independent gates, all required before a cycle acts for real \(em
config
.RI ( auto_calm_enabled ,
off by default), the runtime armed state (toggled only by
.BR ramstein (1)'s
.I autocalm arm / dry
\(em config\-persisted
.RI ( auto_calm_armed ),
surviving a daemon restart once armed: renice and a cgroup
.I memory.high
squeeze are both reversible, unlike kill, so the TTY confirmation on the
first
.I arm
is the consent gate, not something a restart makes you repeat; disarm any
time with
.IR "autocalm dry" ),
and
.B ramstein\-autocalm.timer
being manually enabled (installed, not enabled, same as
.BR ramstein\-update.timer ).
Kill is never reachable from an auto-calm cycle at any setting \(em the
graduated response stops at a desktop notification naming the one
.I calm \-\-kill
command it will not run itself.
.TP
.B The memory.high floor
.I calm \-\-high
clamps its target to at least 1.1x the process's own current RSS \(em a
tampered or fat-fingered size can never be set low enough to
instant-thrash-OOM the process it's meant to calm.
.TP
.B Coexistence
If systemd-oomd or earlyoom is already active, every
.I calm
action and
.I advise
say so; ramstein never races another OOM-fighter \(em its kill prompts
stay advisory information, not an automatic stand-down of the action
itself (that decision is still yours, and still requires the same TTY
confirmation).
